Choose what a model must represent before calculating (@engineering-model-choices: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/foundations/Choose-the-model-and-keep-its-boundary-visible.lingua#r_50DYM9BTTETPVTR2Z7DE3VHK91") { r_0F7FD805EB7379B6E978BD4F6C
A model is a deliberately limited description used to answer a question. List its inputs, state, predicted outputs and assumptions before choosing a formula. An input is supplied to the model. A state describes something retained as the system evolves, such as inventory or position. A predicted output is the answer you derive. A parameter such as an acceptable fraction still needs an interpretation and evidence; giving it a symbol does not make it constant in service.

**Read:** the opening of [Slocum's FUNdaMENTALS](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ) topic 3, including the simple-model discussion on printed pages 3-1 and 3-2. Draw your own reduced representation and explain what was omitted. Later force, deformation, precision and control arguments require their respective foundations. The following shared mechanical-model lesson is first read for the distinction between particle, rigid and deformable descriptions. Describe its lever qualitatively now; defer the assigned equilibrium and bending calculations until forces, moments and elastic response have been developed.

Consider a rigid shelf model. Calling the shelf rigid means deformation is omitted from that model; it does not establish that a real shelf deflects by zero. A force balance can help predict support reactions while leaving displacement unanswered. Likewise an inventory balance accounts for counted objects while leaving cutting forces and label readability unanswered. Use a model appropriate to the question, and keep other requirements visible as unresolved work.

For two sequential stages, define r_A and r_B as maximum processing rates during operation, a_A and a_B as available fractions of scheduled time, and y_A and y_B as accepted fractions of their processed counts. For a fixed interval T, an aggregate no-rework model has upstream acceptable capacity T r_A a_A y_A and downstream processing capacity T r_B a_B. With no initial intermediate stock, the final acceptable count is bounded above by y_B times the smaller of those capacities. This assumes each accepted upstream object supplies one downstream input and that the assigned fractions apply to the modeled stream. It relaxes timing, startup, buffer and other constraints; it is not a construction of a feasible schedule.

With T = 10 h, r_A = 120/h, a_A = 0.9, y_A = 0.95, r_B = 100/h, a_B = 0.95 and y_B = 0.98, the capacities are 1,026 intermediate objects and 950 downstream inputs. The final acceptable upper bound is 931, or 93.1 per scheduled hour. Multiplying both availability factors into a single serial rate would describe a different assumption about simultaneous operation; it is not this buffered aggregate model.

**Work:** identify each parameter's numerator and denominator. Explain why an availability fraction is not automatically a probability, and why an average accepted fraction does not promise the same fraction in every batch. Set downstream availability to zero, then upstream acceptable fraction to zero, and check the limiting predictions. Explain what additional model state is needed for a finite buffer and why making A arbitrarily fast cannot overcome a fixed downstream capacity. Fractional counts in general parameter studies are aggregate relaxations; actual individual cards require integer events.

**Shared physical preparation:** [Separate a prediction from a fitted description](../../physics/core/Separate-a-prediction-from-a-fitted-description.lingua#r_07A97F390AC6E5D127D08C8E42). Reuse [ratios and percentages](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Ratios-and-percentages.lingua#r_2VBAHXE0WMP7E6WA0NNKV1N31S) and [units and dimensions](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1) at the point of need.

